<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Artificial intelligence has advanced significantly through the development of intelligent game-playing systems, providing rigorous testbeds for decision-making, strategic planning, and adaptive learning. However, resource-constrained environments pose critical challenges, as conventional deep learning methods heavily rely on extensive datasets and computational resources. In this paper, we propose a lightweight hybrid framework for the Game of the Amazons, which explores the paradigm of weak-to-strong generalization by integrating the structural reasoning of graph-based learning with the generative capabilities of large language models. Specifically, we leverage a Graph Attention Autoencoder to inform a multi-step Monte Carlo Tree Search, utilize a Stochastic Graph Genetic Algorithm to optimize evaluation signals, and harness GPT-4o-mini to generate synthetic training data. Unlike traditional approaches that rely on expert demonstrations, our framework learns from noisy and imperfect supervision. We demonstrate that the Graph Attention mechanism effectively functions as a structural filter, denoising the LLM’s outputs. Experiments on a 10<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>10 Amazons board show that our hybrid approach not only achieves a 15%–56% improvement in decision accuracy over baselines but also significantly outperforms its teacher model (GPT-4o-mini), achieving a competitive win rate of 45.0% at N=30 nodes and a decisive 66.5% at only N=50 nodes. These results verify the feasibility of evolving specialized, high-performance game AI from general-purpose foundation models under stringent computational constraints.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>
Monte Carlo Tree search; Graph Attention Autoencoder; GPT-4o-mini; Graph Genetic Algorithm

</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With the explosive growth of large language models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, traditional deep-learning approaches—once at the forefront of AI research—have increasingly fallen out of view. Both paradigms require enormous computational resources, and their real-world adoption remains constrained by the need for high-end hardware. Recently, there has been growing interest in deploying decision-making frameworks in resource-constrained or edge environments. For instance, Yang et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> combined multimodal knowledge graphs with LLMs for risk decision-making, while Song et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2025</a>)</cite> explored Deep Reinforcement Learning for model deployment in edge environments. Similarly, Zhu et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> investigated task offloading using LLM-enhanced Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning. However, complex competitive games remain underexplored in this context. If intelligent systems could deliver strong performance on everyday machines, a far broader audience could leverage them, thereby expanding the pool of real<span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>world data and accelerating further research.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To test the ability of agents with large models under resource constraints, we tackle this challenge through the lens of the Game of the Amazons. It is a board game with hundreds of branches, making it an ideal testbed for artificial intelligence algorithms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song and Müller, 2014</a>)</cite>. The rule of placing a barrier after making a move in Game of the Amazons significantly increases the search space. First-player wins were found for the 4x5, 5x4, 4x6, 5x6, and 4x7 boards, while the 6x4 board turned out to be a second-player win <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song and Müller, 2014</a>)</cite>. However, the 10x10 boards remain unsolved within the limited time available, leaving room for further research.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The number of legal moves in standard Amazons positions often reaches into the hundreds or even thousands, placing tremendous strain on any backwards-pruning search strategy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hearn, 2005</a>)</cite>. To tame this combinatorial explosion, Tong et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tong et al., 2019</a>)</cite> exploited a parallelised PVS (Proof-Number Search) framework augmented with a transposition table to cut off irrelevant branches more aggressively. They further refined pruning by assigning each candidate move a historical-heuristic score, which markedly improved search efficiency. Dehghani et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dehghani and Babamir, 2017</a>)</cite> proposed a genetic<span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>algorithm (GA) approach for search<span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>space reduction. Their experiments demonstrated that, whereas the runtime of traditional Min<span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text">-</span>Max grows exponentially with search depth, the GA<span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text">-</span>based method scales much more gently and overtakes Min<span id="S1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text">-</span>Max performance once trees exceed a moderate depth threshold. However, their reliance on a relatively simple evaluation function may fail to capture the nuanced structure of complex board states.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Although the movement range in Game of the Amazons is limited to a 10x10 grid, its unique movement rules result in a search space that can rival that of Go. It is also the reason why Game of the Amazons is chosen. The Game of the Amazons is played on a square grid, featuring two distinct sets of pieces for each player. Each player controls four pieces. For each step, a player should move one of the four pieces to a grid that is in a straight line from the current grid, and then place a barrier that blocks movement according to the same rule. The objective of the game is to restrict the opponent’s movement until no piece can move. Consequently, machines need to consider two factors when making a move:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Machines must be careful about the move that is optimal for restricting the opponent’s move. In some cases, this move will put our other pieces in jeopardy, while in other cases, such a sacrifice is beneficial to winning the whole game. Deciding on numerous choices is key for machines to learn, as there is no straightforward construction for most of these values <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Snatzke, 2004</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">They need to predict the implications of every move. In addition to the current board information, any future board information can be crucial to winning the entire game. Thus, the opponent’s move should be taken into consideration. Such an operation can be implemented by searching. However, as the search depth increases, the cost of searching will become exponential. Now, a question is raised: Is it possible to achieve good results without searching for deeper nodes?</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) algorithm is widely used in various intelligent agent game scenarios and has attracted widespread attention from researchers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Świechowski et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moon et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Crippa et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. The number of legal moves in standard Amazons positions often reaches into the hundreds or even thousands, placing tremendous strain on any backwards-pruning search strategy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hearn, 2005</a>)</cite>. To tame this combinatorial explosion, Tong et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tong et al., 2019</a>)</cite> exploited a parallelised PVS (Proof-Number Search) framework augmented with a transposition table to cut off irrelevant branches more aggressively. They further refined pruning by assigning each candidate move a historical-heuristic score, which markedly improved search efficiency. Dehghani et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dehghani and Babamir, 2017</a>)</cite> proposed a genetic<span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>algorithm (GA) approach for search<span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>space reduction. Their experiments demonstrated that, whereas the runtime of traditional Min<span id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text">-</span>Max grows exponentially with search depth, the GA<span id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text">-</span>based method scales much more gently and overtakes Min<span id="S1.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text">-</span>Max performance once trees exceed a moderate depth threshold. However, their reliance on a relatively simple evaluation function may fail to capture the nuanced structure of complex board states. Besides the use of primitive UCTS, Fu et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al., 2018</a>)</cite> put forward a staged UCT(Upper Confidence Bound Apply to Tree) algorithm. In their work, Linear Discriminant Analysis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fisher, 1936</a>)</cite> was first used to compress information from feature points on a 10x10 chessboard into a two-dimensional space. Then, the K-means algorithm divided the stages of the game. Such algorithm enabled different weights.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Additionally, in the study of computer games, estimating the game situation is another key focus. Jianbo et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jianbo et al., 2019</a>)</cite> combined convolutional neural networks with reinforcement learning to create a comprehensive representation space. The former trained convolutional neural networks with eight 10x10 layers, and the latter considered nine 10x10 layers. What makes it different is that the latter utilised the k-average clustering algorithm to divide chess stages and thus achieved a better result. Both networks were trained by reinforcement learning. However, it lost its interpretability. Kagkas et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kagkas et al., 2023</a>)</cite> proposed an estimator based on a radial basis neural network for the high-dimensional representation of the game situation, which ensured the robustness of the valuation. Hsueh et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hsueh and Ikeda, 2024</a>)</cite> proposed a new situation-based smoothing function to calculate move scores to solve the problem of Go advantage controlling scores. Deep learning and reinforcement learning approaches have shown promise in Game of the Amazons <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jianbo et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, but they suffer from a lack of interpretability and impose stringent requirements on data quality. In particular, because Game of the Amazons is relatively obscure, historical game records are scarce, making it difficult to learn reliable value functions. Moreover, the evaluation of any given position is inherently non-fixed, so one must transform the underlying score matrix: linear mappings struggle to capture a sufficiently rich feature space, while nonlinear mappings often obscure interpretability. Finally, by relying almost exclusively on neural-network-based inference, these methods downplay human expertise and risk overfitting to their own limited experience. Zhang et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2021</a>)</cite> split the neural network into a rule network and a skill network, thereby improving the learning speed and alleviating the severe dependence on computing resources.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">To address these challenges, we present a novel MCTS framework tailored to the Game of the Amazons. As a foundation, we replace conventional exhaustive search trees with a Monte Carlo Tree structure, intrinsically reducing computational overhead. Building on this baseline, we introduce a multi-round, depth-focused search strategy that curbs the exponential growth of the tree—allowing us to probe deeper nodes without incurring the cost of exhaustive breadth searches. Accurate state evaluation is essential for this targeted approach, so we incorporate two lightweight autoencoder layers—one for movement steps and one for placement steps—to refine the evaluation parameters with minimal added latency. Next, we enhance candidate selection from both stochastic and structural perspectives: the Graph-Attention Autoencoder (GAT-AE) captures MCTS-generated graph structure via a graph-attention autoencoder, while the Stochastic Graph Genetic Algorithm (SGGA) injects controlled randomness into candidate sampling. Finally, to train these components without relying on human game logs, we generate synthetic data using large language models and introduce an update mechanism that decouples objective values from their origins. Empirical results demonstrate that our algorithm’s performance is insensitive to search depth—effectively resolving the traditional trade-off between depth and candidate quality—and offers a fresh perspective on tree-based candidate selection.In summary, our contributions can be summarized as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A novel general architecture. We build a novel architecture that allows for transferability. In detail, the game of Amazons can simulate real-time decision-making processes in reality and assist humans in making decisions. Our model abstracts players’ assets or actions as pieces on a chessboard and allows for the selection of influencing factors. This approach combines traditional deep learning with objective function methods, making our model more interpretable than conventional deep learning and more accurate than manually constructed objective function methods. Besides, our model is capable of capturing the structure of the tree from both the perspective of the neighboring nodes and the overall view.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A shift in perspective. Generally speaking, deeper and larger node searches can lead to higher accuracy, but they also result in significantly increased search time. Our model addresses this issue by employing methods such as multi-round searches and demonstrates that, under certain conditions, incorporating techniques from machine learning and deep learning can yield satisfactory results.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Validation of Weak-to-Strong Generalization.</span> Unlike traditional methods relying on expert data, we explore the paradigm of “weak-to-strong generalization” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Burns et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. We utilize a general-purpose LLM (GPT-4o-mini) as a <span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">weak supervisor</span> to generate noisy training signals. Our experiments demonstrate that the proposed Graph Attention mechanism effectively functions as an information bottleneck, filtering out the supervisor’s errors (hallucinations) while retaining structural strategies. This proves that our framework can evolve a specialized “strong student” model from a “weak teacher,” offering a viable path for domains lacking expert data.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we first define the problem of the Game of the Amazons. Then, we introduce the design of the evaluation function.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Game of Amazons Rules</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Amazon, invented by Walter Zamkauskas in 1988, was played on a square board, specifically 10x10 in this work, with each player owing four amazons. The Game of the Amazons can be formalized as a triplet <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\langle C_{w},C_{b},B\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\langle C_{w},C_{b},B\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{w}=[C_{w_{1}},C_{w_{2}},C_{w_{3}},C_{w_{4}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{w}=[C_{w_{1}},C_{w_{2}},C_{w_{3}},C_{w_{4}}]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a list of pieces, each <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{w_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{w_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> representing the positional information of the <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> white piece. Similarly, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{b}=[C_{b_{1}},C_{b_{2}},C_{b_{3}},C_{b_{4}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{b}=[C_{b_{1}},C_{b_{2}},C_{b_{3}},C_{b_{4}}]</annotation></semantics></math> specifies the positional information of the black pieces, while <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> represents the set of all barrier positions on the board. At an intermediate turn in the game, the board configuration is shown in yboard, where <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">X</span> denotes a barrier and the black and white pieces represent the two players’ pieces.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.10512v1/board.jpg" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:191/190;" width="191" height="190" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 1: </span>The Board Configuration of One Turn.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The state of the board at the <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> turn is denoted as <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the sequence of board states from the start of the game up to the current turn is expressed as <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{i}=[s_{0},s_{1},\dots,s_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{i}=[s_{0},s_{1},\dots,s_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math>. In each turn, players alternately move one of their four pieces and subsequently place a barrier. The game terminates when a player is unable to execute a valid move, resulting in their loss. The objective is to determine the final sequence of states, <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that no further updates to the board state are possible.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Autoencoder(AE)</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The structure of autoencoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Michelucci, 2022</a>)</cite> consists of two parts: an encoder and a decoder. The encoder maps the input data <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> to features <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math> in the latent space, and the decoder decodes it back to <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>, making it as close to the original input as possible. Its structure is shown in Fig.<a href="#S2.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ 2.2 Autoencoder(AE) ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Resource-constrained Amazons chess decision framework integrating large language models and graph attention This work was supported by the National Key Research and Development Project of China under Grant (No. 2020YFA0714300), National Natural Science Foundation of China under Grant (No. 61833005 and 12061088), the Open Project of Key Laboratory of Transport Industry of Comprehensive Transportation Theory (Nanjing Modern Multimodal Transportation Laboratory) under Grant MTF2023004, the China Postdoctoral Science Foundation under Grant 2024T170129 and Grant GZC20240261." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.10512v1/autoencoder.jpg" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:191/73;" width="191" height="73" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 2: </span>The Structure of Autoencoder Model.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Autoencoder is to learn the functions <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A:R^{n}\rightarrow R^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A:R^{n}\rightarrow R^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> (encoder) and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B:R^{p}\rightarrow R^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B:R^{p}\rightarrow R^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> (decoder) that satisfy:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle c\arg\min_{A,B}\mathbb{E}\left[\Delta\left(\mathbf{x},B(A(\mathbf{x}))\right)\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle c\arg\min_{A,B}\mathbb{E}\left[\Delta\left(\mathbf{x},B(A(\mathbf{x}))\right)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔼</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the expectation over the input distribution, and <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is the reconstruction loss function, typically set to the <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bank et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. The input and output layer has a dimension of 5 to represent the 5 measures we design, which will be introduced in the following paragraph. The hidden layer has a dimension of 3, which is chosen based on our experience.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Graph Attention Networks (GAT) </h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GAT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Veličković et al., 2018</a>)</cite> was developed after Graph Convoluted Network(GCN), which is unable to solve inductive problems. Typically, GAT consists of global graph attention or mask graph attention. In our work, the later one was opted, which making full use of the graph’s structure. Compared to vectors, graphs have the advantage of being able to represent complex relationships and connections between data points in a more dynamic and flexible manner.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A weight matrix W and <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, a function mapping high-dimension concatenated features into a real number, are two vital parameters. They compute attention coefficients <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating the importance of node j’s features to node i’s:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle e_{ij}=\alpha(\textbf{W}\vec{h_{i}},\textbf{W}\vec{h_{j}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">W</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">W</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle e_{ij}=\alpha(\textbf{W}\vec{h_{i}},\textbf{W}\vec{h_{j}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is a shared attentional mechanism and also a single-layer feedforward neural network, such as LeakyReLU activation. To make coefficients easily comparable across different nodes, we normalize them across all choices of j using the softmax function <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Veličković et al., 2018</a>)</cite>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\alpha_{ij}={\textbf{softmax}_{j}}(e_{ij})=\frac{exp(e_{ij})}{\sum\limits_{k\in\mathbf{N}_{i}}exp(e_{ik})}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">softmax</mtext><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐍</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\alpha_{ij}={\textbf{softmax}_{j}}(e_{ij})=\frac{exp(e_{ij})}{\sum\limits_{k\in\mathbf{N}_{i}}exp(e_{ik})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To obtain <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{h_{i}^{{}^{\prime}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{h_{i}^{{}^{\prime}}}</annotation></semantics></math> , we still take advantage of W and <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\vec{h_{i}^{{}^{\prime}}}=\sigma(\sum\limits_{j\in\mathbf{N}_{i}}\alpha_{ij}^{k}\textbf{W}\vec{h_{j}})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐍</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">W</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\vec{h_{i}^{{}^{\prime}}}=\sigma(\sum\limits_{j\in\mathbf{N}_{i}}\alpha_{ij}^{k}\textbf{W}\vec{h_{j}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> aggregates features from each head, <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{h_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{h_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the input of node j while <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{h_{i}^{{}^{\prime}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{h_{i}^{{}^{\prime}}}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the output of node i. In our work, input is a 1x5 tensor generated from the output of the AutoEncoder and output is a value ranging from 0 to 1, representing the quality of the move from the worst to the best. 8 attention heads are used in this model.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>Design of the Evaluation Function</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Manually designed evaluation measures, while unable to fully capture the intricacies of the representation space, provide a basis for further refinement. This study employs four measures inspired by value and knowledge domains, in addition to a metric adapted from previous research. These measures are described below:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Adjacency-Territory</span>:
This measure computes the ratio of squares accessible to a player’s pieces compared to those accessible to their opponent’s. A square is considered "occupied" by the white pieces if it can be reached more quickly by them, adhering to the rule that movement occurs to an adjacent square in each step.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Line-Territory</span>:
Similar to adjacency-territory, this measure evaluates the ratio of accessible squares. However, in this case, a square is considered "occupied" by the white pieces if it can be reached more quickly by them when movement occurs in a straight line in each step.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">One-Mobility</span>:
This metric quantifies the number of adjacent squares that the moved piece can reach in its immediate vicinity. The value is normalized by dividing it by 8, ensuring it falls within the range <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Line-Mobility</span>:
This measure calculates the number of reachable squares for all four of a player’s pieces after placing a barrier. To normalize the metric, the value is divided by 4, yielding a range <math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Position</span>:
The position metric is defined as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.I1.i5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p=\begin{cases}\sum\limits_{A}\left(2^{D_{1}^{1}(A)-D_{1}^{2}(A)}\right),&amp;\text{if }\text{turn}\leq 30,\\
\sum\limits_{A}\left(2^{D_{2}^{1}(A)-D_{2}^{2}(A)}\right),&amp;\text{if }\text{turn}&gt;30,\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>A</mi></munder><mrow><mo lspace="0em">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mtext>turn</mtext></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>A</mi></munder><mrow><mo lspace="0em">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mtext>turn</mtext></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p=\begin{cases}\sum\limits_{A}\left(2^{D_{1}^{1}(A)-D_{1}^{2}(A)}\right),&amp;\text{if }\text{turn}\leq 30,\\
\sum\limits_{A}\left(2^{D_{2}^{1}(A)-D_{2}^{2}(A)}\right),&amp;\text{if }\text{turn}&gt;30,\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.I1.i5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where this formulation is adapted from the work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lieberum, 2005</a>)</cite>. Here, <math id="S2.I1.i5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> represents the minimum number of steps required for any piece to reach square <math id="S2.I1.i5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> under the rule of straight-line movement, and <math id="S2.I1.i5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{2}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the minimum steps required under adjacent-square movement. The superscripts <math id="S2.I1.i5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I1.i5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> refer to the white and black sides, respectively.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Collectively, these evaluation measures aim to quantify positional and territorial advantages, enabling a comprehensive assessment of board states.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>General Architecture</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Overall framework</h3>

<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.10512v1/framework.jpg" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:357/148;" width="357" height="148" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 3: </span>Overall framework of the proposed method.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our work is divided into two stages. The first stage is a training stage, where UCT-AE model and GAT-AE model are trained using the data generated by large language models with the help of stochastic graph genetic algorithm. After training, two models are utilized in the second stage, the application stage. More details will be discussed in the next section. We suppose that these two models are complementary stochastically and structurally in that order, which will thus improve the overall performance. To validate our assumption, we mainly design competition experiments, including any model of our hybrid model and GPT-4o-mini <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2024</a>)</cite>, the data provider Fig.<a href="#S3.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ 3.1 Overall framework ‣ 3 General Architecture ‣ Resource-constrained Amazons chess decision framework integrating large language models and graph attention This work was supported by the National Key Research and Development Project of China under Grant (No. 2020YFA0714300), National Natural Science Foundation of China under Grant (No. 61833005 and 12061088), the Open Project of Key Laboratory of Transport Industry of Comprehensive Transportation Theory (Nanjing Modern Multimodal Transportation Laboratory) under Grant MTF2023004, the China Postdoctoral Science Foundation under Grant 2024T170129 and Grant GZC20240261." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> depicts the overall framework of the proposed method. The pseudo-code of our main framework is also provided in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.1 Overall framework ‣ 3 General Architecture ‣ Resource-constrained Amazons chess decision framework integrating large language models and graph attention This work was supported by the National Key Research and Development Project of China under Grant (No. 2020YFA0714300), National Natural Science Foundation of China under Grant (No. 61833005 and 12061088), the Open Project of Key Laboratory of Transport Industry of Comprehensive Transportation Theory (Nanjing Modern Multimodal Transportation Laboratory) under Grant MTF2023004, the China Postdoctoral Science Foundation under Grant 2024T170129 and Grant GZC20240261." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>AI Move and Placement Strategy (MCTS + SGGA + GAT<span id="S3.T1.3" class="ltx_text">-</span>AE)</figcaption>
<table id="S3.T1.4" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S3.T1.4.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.4.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> AI Move and Placement Strategy</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.4.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.4.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.4.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> Initial board state <math id="S3.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, time limit <math id="S3.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>, SGGA crossover rate <math id="S3.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.4.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.4.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="S3.T1.4.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output:</span> Selected move <math id="S3.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{*}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.4.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.4.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">1 <span id="S3.T1.4.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Initialize MCTS tree <math id="S3.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> root(<math id="S3.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.4.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.4.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">2 <span id="S3.T1.4.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Load Autoencoder on CPU; Load GAT on GPU</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.4.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.4.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">3 <math id="S3.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\leftarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\leftarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.T1.4.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">, start timer, set mode <math id="S3.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> (BLACK/WHITE)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.4.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.4.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">4 <span id="S3.T1.4.7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">while<span id="S3.T1.4.7.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="S3.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&lt;T_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&lt;T_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> </span>do</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.4.8" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.4.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">5  <math id="S3.T1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.T1.4.8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter"> UCT-AE_Select(<math id="S3.T1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.4.9" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.4.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">6  <span id="S3.T1.4.9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Update board and tree: <math id="S3.T1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}\leftarrow\mathcal{B}\cup n_{\text{new}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">new</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}\leftarrow\mathcal{B}\cup n_{\text{new}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.T1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> insert(<math id="S3.T1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T},n_{\text{new}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">new</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T},n_{\text{new}}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.4.10" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.4.10.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">7  <math id="S3.T1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.T1.4.10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter"> elapsed()</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.4.11" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.4.11.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">8 <span id="S3.T1.4.11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">end while</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.4.12" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.4.12.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">9 <span id="S3.T1.4.12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Perform SGGA:</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.4.13" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.4.13.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">10  <math id="S3.T1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\text{new}}\leftarrow\mathrm{SGGA}(\sigma,\mathcal{T},\mathcal{COUNTER})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">new</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>SGGA</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\text{new}}\leftarrow\mathrm{SGGA}(\sigma,\mathcal{T},\mathcal{COUNTER})</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.4.14" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.4.14.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">11  <span id="S3.T1.4.14.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">repeat</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.4.15" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.4.15.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">12   <math id="S3.T1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\text{new}}\leftarrow\mathrm{SGGA}(\sigma,n_{\text{new}},\mathcal{COUNTER})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">new</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>SGGA</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">new</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\text{new}}\leftarrow\mathrm{SGGA}(\sigma,n_{\text{new}},\mathcal{COUNTER})</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.4.16" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.4.16.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">13  <math id="S3.T1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textbf{until}\ NodeCount(n_{\text{new}})\geq 2^{(\text{Height}_{\max}-NodeHeight(n_{\text{new}})+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">until</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">new</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">Height</mtext><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">new</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{until}\ NodeCount(n_{\text{new}})\geq 2^{(\text{Height}_{\max}-NodeHeight(n_{\text{new}})+1)}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.4.17" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.4.17.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">        <span id="S3.T1.4.17.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">or</span> <math id="S3.T1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{COUNTER}\geq 50000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>50000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{COUNTER}\geq 50000</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.4.18" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.4.18.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">14 <span id="S3.T1.4.18.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Choose node <math id="S3.T1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{*}=n_{\text{new}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">new</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{*}=n_{\text{new}}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S3.T1.4.18.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">or</span> <span id="S3.T1.4.18.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_bold">None</span></span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.4.19" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.4.19.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">15 <span id="S3.T1.4.19.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Construct subgraph <math id="S3.T1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,A)\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,A)\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> ExtractGraph(<math id="S3.T1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.4.20" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.4.20.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">16 <math id="S3.T1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\leftarrow\mathrm{GAT\text{-}AE}(X,A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>GAT</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>AE</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\leftarrow\mathrm{GAT\text{-}AE}(X,A)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.4.21" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.4.21.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">17 <math id="S3.T1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{*}\leftarrow\arg\max_{i}\,y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{*}\leftarrow\arg\max_{i}\,y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.4.22" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.4.22.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">18 <span id="S3.T1.4.22.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">return<span id="S3.T1.4.22.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Decision_Strategy(<math id="S3.T1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>,<math id="S3.T1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></span></td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Training Stage</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Traditionally, the evaluation function is constrained to its human-made representation if the training resource is limited. In this work, our goal is to reverse this trend by integrating it with our hybrid model so that the intelligent system can be greatly improved. We first introduce MCTS with an update mechanism. Then, three models are introduced sequentially in detail. Finally, how we generate data by using GPT-4o-mini, with SGGA augmented, is introduced.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1 </span>Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) with Update Mechanism</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The MCTS algorithm starts from the current state and gradually builds a game tree. Each iteration includes four steps: selection, expansion, simulation, and backtracking. The specific process is shown in Fig.<a href="#S3.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ 3.2.1 Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) with Update Mechanism ‣ 3.2 Training Stage ‣ 3 General Architecture ‣ Resource-constrained Amazons chess decision framework integrating large language models and graph attention This work was supported by the National Key Research and Development Project of China under Grant (No. 2020YFA0714300), National Natural Science Foundation of China under Grant (No. 61833005 and 12061088), the Open Project of Key Laboratory of Transport Industry of Comprehensive Transportation Theory (Nanjing Modern Multimodal Transportation Laboratory) under Grant MTF2023004, the China Postdoctoral Science Foundation under Grant 2024T170129 and Grant GZC20240261." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.10512v1/UCT_1.png" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:286/83;" width="286" height="83" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 4: </span>The Structure of MCTS.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The selection step is to start from the root node and select the most promising child node according to a certain strategy until a node that is not fully expanded is reached. The expansion step is to randomly select one from the unexplored child nodes of the node and add it to the game tree. The simulation step is to start from the node and randomly play the game until a termination condition is met. The backtracking step is to start from the node and backtrack up along the game tree to update the number of visits and cumulative rewards of each node. When the predetermined number of iterations or time limit is reached, the algorithm is ended and one MCT is successfully constructed. The advantages of the MCTS algorithm are general, adaptive, and controllable while the disadvantages are low search efficiency and some of the nodes searched are useless.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Hayes et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hayes et al., 2022</a>)</cite> proposed DMCTS(Distributional MCTS), maintaining a posterior distribution over value estimates at each node and employing Thompson sampling to draw a sample from each node’s posterior distribution. Inspired by this distributional perspective and thinking that the quality of data can not be ensured, we hypothesized that deeper nodes may accumulate greater variance due to compounding estimation errors, noting that deeper nodes in tree search are inherently derived from shallower ones. Thus, instead of leveraging the distribution constructed by DMCTS, we proposed a global depth normalization mechanism that rescales each node’s value relative to its depth. This normalization aligns nodes from different layers onto a comparable value scale, with the objective value of any node normalized to the [0,1] range, mitigating the effect of depth-induced noise and promoting fairer evaluation across the tree.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, we introduced a two-stage recursive value-propagation scheme for rooted trees, designed to encode both depth-dependent nonlinear accumulation and global depth normalization. Let each node <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> carry an objective value <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{obj}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑜𝑏𝑗</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{obj}(n)</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{height}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℎ𝑒𝑖𝑔ℎ𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{height}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> denote its depth (root at height 1), with the overall maximum depth of the tree denoted <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{H}_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{H}_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>. The update proceeds in two phases:</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">1. Depth-Dependent Accumulation (Pass I)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Starting from the leaves and proceeding upward, each internal node <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> computes the arithmetic mean of its children’s states from upper layers to lower layers alternatively,</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bar{v}(n)=\frac{1}{|\mathit{Ch}(n)|}\sum_{c\in\mathit{Ch}(n)}\mathit{obj}(c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶ℎ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶ℎ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝑜𝑏𝑗</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bar{v}(n)=\frac{1}{|\mathit{Ch}(n)|}\sum_{c\in\mathit{Ch}(n)}\mathit{obj}(c)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Since in board game simulations, nodes at successive depths alternate between the agent and the opponent, we then updates its own state according to the parity of its nesting depth:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathit{obj}(n)\leftarrow\begin{cases}\mathit{obj}(n)+\bar{v}(n),&amp;\text{if }\mathit{height}(n)\text{ is even},\\
\mathit{obj}(n)+2^{-\bar{v}(n)},&amp;\text{if }\mathit{height}(n)\text{ is odd}\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑜𝑏𝑗</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑜𝑏𝑗</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℎ𝑒𝑖𝑔ℎ𝑡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> is even</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑜𝑏𝑗</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℎ𝑒𝑖𝑔ℎ𝑡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> is odd</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathit{obj}(n)\leftarrow\begin{cases}\mathit{obj}(n)+\bar{v}(n),&amp;\text{if }\mathit{height}(n)\text{ is even},\\
\mathit{obj}(n)+2^{-\bar{v}(n)},&amp;\text{if }\mathit{height}(n)\text{ is odd}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{-\bar{v}(n)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{-\bar{v}(n)}</annotation></semantics></math> ensures that when the opponent node’s objective value is 0, the parent node receives a full reward of 1 while the value is 1, the parent node will still receive a reward of 0.5. This design aims to encourage the model to place greater emphasis on constraining the behavior of opponent nodes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">2. Global Depth Normalization (Pass II)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Once all nodes have accumulated their depth-dependent contributions, a second top-down traversal rescales each node’s final state by:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathit{obj}(n)\leftarrow\frac{\mathit{obj}(n)}{\mathit{H}_{\max}+1-\mathit{height}(n)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑜𝑏𝑗</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>𝑜𝑏𝑗</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ℎ𝑒𝑖𝑔ℎ𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathit{obj}(n)\leftarrow\frac{\mathit{obj}(n)}{\mathit{H}_{\max}+1-\mathit{height}(n)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This normalization introduces a <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">depth-based penalty</span> to mitigate the risk of error propagation in deep searches. Since evaluation scores at deeper nodes rely on longer chains of simulations, they inherently carry higher variance and uncertainty. By scaling the objective value inversely with depth, we effectively implement a <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">linear discount factor</span>. This mechanism prioritizes reliable, near-term rewards over distant, uncertain projections, preventing the MCTS from over-committing to deep branches based on potentially flawed simulations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This two-pass mechanism simultaneously (a) injects nonlinear, depth-sensitive weighting factors into local updates and (b) enforces a consistent global normalization so that deeper and shallower nodes remain directly comparable. This benefits SGGA by fairly selecting the next generations.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2 </span>UCT algorithm combined with autoencoder (UCT-AE)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To construct the UCT, we utilize traditional MCTS algorithm without requiring any prior knowledge or evaluation function, but only the rules of the game.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The UCT algorithm improves the MCTS algorithm by introducing a strategy for balancing exploration and exploitation, thereby improving the efficiency of step selection. This strategy calculates a confidence upper bound based on the average reward and the number of visits to each node to measure the potential of each node. Then the child node with the highest confidence upper bound is selected in order to find the optimal action.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In our work, the input consists of a <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 5</annotation></semantics></math> tensor <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, representing five measures in the objective function at the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\text{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\text{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> turn. The input tensor is first processed by a linear layer that transforms the 5-dimensional space into a 3-dimensional latent space, followed by a ReLU activation function to introduce nonlinearity. The resulting <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 3</annotation></semantics></math> tensor is then passed through a second linear layer, which maps the 3-dimensional space back to a 5-dimensional space, and is subsequently subjected to a Tanh activation function. This sequence of transformations produces a <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 5</annotation></semantics></math> output tensor <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}</annotation></semantics></math>’ = <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{AE}(\mathbf{v})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐀𝐄</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{AE}(\mathbf{v})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Due to the two-step rule that differentiates our approach from other board games, two distinct autoencoders are employed, denoted as <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{AE}=[\text{AE}_{1},\text{AE}_{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐀𝐄</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>AE</mtext><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mtext>AE</mtext><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{AE}=[\text{AE}_{1},\text{AE}_{2}]</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponding to these operations. Softmax normalization was applied to enhance the randomness of simulations, thereby enabling the simulation of diverse scenarios. Subsequent experiments demonstrate the effectiveness of this modification.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We prioritize simplicity by avoiding overly complex models, as increased model complexity would significantly extend the search time of one node, then accumulating. Thus, the UCT-AE algorithm, an approach combining Monte Carlo Tree with autoencoder, is utilized to simulate players’ moves in our work. To determine which move to simulate, an objective function is incorporated. The modified Upper Confidence Bound (UCB) of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{\text{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>j</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{\text{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> node expression is as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{UCB}_{j}=\mathbf{AE}_{1}(\mathbf{v}_{j})\mathbf{W}_{1}+\sqrt{\frac{2\ln n}{n_{j}+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐔𝐂𝐁</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀𝐄</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{UCB}_{j}=\mathbf{AE}_{1}(\mathbf{v}_{j})\mathbf{W}_{1}+\sqrt{\frac{2\ln n}{n_{j}+1}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{AE}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀𝐄</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{AE}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the first autoencoder for movement, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the five measures of the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{\text{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>j</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{\text{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> node, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of simulations, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of times the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{\text{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>j</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{\text{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> arm has been selected. This formulation effectively balances exploitation and exploration.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In designing the objective function, traditional method typically relied on experience and prior research findings. However, this approach requires researchers to understand the intrinsic principles of the Game of Amazons, making it challenging to achieve comparable accuracy. To address this, autoencoders are used to remap the input, enhancing feature representation capabilities and modifying the objective function. The overall value is computed as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v_{j}=\alpha\cdot\mathbf{AE}_{1}(\mathbf{v}_{j})\mathbf{W}_{1}+(1-\alpha)\cdot\mathbf{AE}_{2}(\mathbf{v}_{j})\mathbf{W}_{2}+\sqrt{\frac{2\ln n}{n_{j}+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>𝐀𝐄</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>𝐀𝐄</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v_{j}=\alpha\cdot\mathbf{AE}_{1}(\mathbf{v}_{j})\mathbf{W}_{1}+(1-\alpha)\cdot\mathbf{AE}_{2}(\mathbf{v}_{j})\mathbf{W}_{2}+\sqrt{\frac{2\ln n}{n_{j}+1}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is an adjustable parameter.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.3 </span>Graph Attention Networks based on AutoEncoder model(GAT-AE)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fig.<a href="#S3.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ 3.2.3 Graph Attention Networks based on AutoEncoder model(GAT-AE) ‣ 3.2 Training Stage ‣ 3 General Architecture ‣ Resource-constrained Amazons chess decision framework integrating large language models and graph attention This work was supported by the National Key Research and Development Project of China under Grant (No. 2020YFA0714300), National Natural Science Foundation of China under Grant (No. 61833005 and 12061088), the Open Project of Key Laboratory of Transport Industry of Comprehensive Transportation Theory (Nanjing Modern Multimodal Transportation Laboratory) under Grant MTF2023004, the China Postdoctoral Science Foundation under Grant 2024T170129 and Grant GZC20240261." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> illustrates the information flow into the GAT-AE model. Typically, GAT requires both an adjacency matrix and a node feature matrix. To minimize redundancy, the four root nodes are temporarily aggregated into a single super-node. Let us denote the input as <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math>; the GAT-AE first processes it via Eq.<a href="#S3.E10" title="In 3.2.2 UCT algorithm combined with autoencoder (UCT-AE) ‣ 3.2 Training Stage ‣ 3 General Architecture ‣ Resource-constrained Amazons chess decision framework integrating large language models and graph attention This work was supported by the National Key Research and Development Project of China under Grant (No. 2020YFA0714300), National Natural Science Foundation of China under Grant (No. 61833005 and 12061088), the Open Project of Key Laboratory of Transport Industry of Comprehensive Transportation Theory (Nanjing Modern Multimodal Transportation Laboratory) under Grant MTF2023004, the China Postdoctoral Science Foundation under Grant 2024T170129 and Grant GZC20240261." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>. After the graph attention layers, the output is passed through a shifted activation function:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textbf{O}=f(v_{j})=\frac{\tanh(v_{j})+1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">O</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>tanh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textbf{O}=f(v_{j})=\frac{\tanh(v_{j})+1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_bold">O</span> represents the normalized output of the GAT-AE, mapping raw scores to the interval <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. We utilize this formulation to leverage the <span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">high sensitivity</span> of the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tanh" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>tanh</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tanh</annotation></semantics></math> function around the origin. Unlike a standard linear mapping, this non-linear transformation creates a <span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">sharp decision boundary</span>, pushing the output values towards saturation (0 or 1). This is crucial for the subsequent Stochastic Graph Genetic Algorithm (SGGA), as it provides clear, binary-like distinction signals between favorable and unfavorable moves, thereby enhancing the <span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">selection pressure</span> during the evolutionary optimization process without requiring gradient-based updates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our hybrid model combines GAT with SGGA to select the next step from stochastic and structural perspective. Thus, the probability distribution can be grasped so that the state of the board is visualized by the count of the node, rewards, to analyze which piece is superior further.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.10512v1/GAT.jpg" id="S3.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:286/174;" width="286" height="174" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 5: </span>The Structure of Autoencoder Model.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.4 </span>Stochastic Graph Genetic Algorithm (SGGA)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Traditional MCTS constructs a tree involving several nodes. SGGA first connects four head nodes, representing four Amazons on the board, together with undirected edges, so that the Monte Carlo Tree becomes a graph. Next, the genetic algorithm was born out of necessity to decompose the score of each node into a probability distribution. In detail, it goes through Selection, Mutation, and Crossover:</p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Selection</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our selection module ensures system execution by sampling two candidate nodes from a genetic repository according to a softmax distribution. This repository stores the history of all generated nodes, and thus the initialization of it must include two nodes. Without the selection state, it will be too slow to reach the expected node.
·</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mutation</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our mutation is also named after biased random walk <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grover and Leskovec, 2016</a>)</cite>. When applied to current node, the subsequent node selected ranges from its children as well as its parent node with mutation step Size <math id="S3.I1.i2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=0.8</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating that the subsequent node is likely to be the parent node of the current one with the possibility of 0.2 or the children node of the current one with the possibility of 0.8. Due to the connection of four head nodes, the subsequent nodes may diverge into different trajectories, resulting in an increase in the node count. Then the subsequent node becomes the next generation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Crossover</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our crossover occurs when two candidates are located at the same position. When it takes place, only one candidate will be additionally added to the genetic repository.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The system will continue operating until the count of any node meets:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textbf{NodeCount}\;\geq\ 2^{\bigl(\textbf{Heightmax}-\textbf{NodeHeight}+1\bigr)}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">NodeCount</mtext><mo lspace="0.558em">≥</mo><msup><mn> 2</mn><mrow><mo maxsize="1.710em" minsize="1.710em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">Heightmax</mtext><mo>−</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">NodeHeight</mtext></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.710em" minsize="1.710em">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textbf{NodeCount}\;\geq\ 2^{\bigl(\textbf{Heightmax}-\textbf{NodeHeight}+1\bigr)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <span id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NodeCount</span> is the count of a certain node, <span id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Heightmax</span> is the maximum height of every node, and <span id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NodeHeight</span> refers to the height of a certain node. The population size ranges from 2 to 50000, meaning that we select two head nodes as initial generations, and if only one node is explored during MCTS, this node will be copied. The upper bound of the number of generations is 50000 in case of a dead end. After selecting the targeted node, we finally trace back to the trajectory of the target node to get the next movement and placement. You can quickly grasp it by reading Fig.<a href="#S3.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ 3.2.4 Stochastic Graph Genetic Algorithm (SGGA) ‣ 3.2 Training Stage ‣ 3 General Architecture ‣ Resource-constrained Amazons chess decision framework integrating large language models and graph attention This work was supported by the National Key Research and Development Project of China under Grant (No. 2020YFA0714300), National Natural Science Foundation of China under Grant (No. 61833005 and 12061088), the Open Project of Key Laboratory of Transport Industry of Comprehensive Transportation Theory (Nanjing Modern Multimodal Transportation Laboratory) under Grant MTF2023004, the China Postdoctoral Science Foundation under Grant 2024T170129 and Grant GZC20240261." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Altarabichi et al. e<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Altarabichi et al., 2023</a>)</cite> embedded the surrogate model into the GA loop to create CHC_QX, showing that CHC_QX converges faster and selects feature subsets that yield noticeably higher classification accuracy. Although this work focuses on feature selection, it reviews how GA<span id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>based instance selection by choosing the most informative training samples can reduce dataset size and improve classifier performance. Similarly, Pongthanisorn and Capi <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pongthanisorn and Capi, 2024</a>)</cite> leveraged a genetic algorithm to optimize data quality, which improved classification accuracy and reduced training time.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">While GA<span id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>based methods can accelerate training and improve model performance by selecting high<span id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>quality samples, they have not been evaluated on training datasets with unpredictable data quality. In contrast, our work is the first to demonstrate the feasibility of SGGA for selecting LLM-generated training data of uncontrolled quality to train deep learning models.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.10512v1/SGGA.jpg" id="S3.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:333/241;" width="333" height="241" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 6: </span>The structure of Stochastic Graph Genetic Algorithm.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.5 </span>Training Data Generation</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Nadas et al.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nadas et al., 2025</a>)</cite> provide evidence that in resource-constrained settings, fine<span id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>tuned LLMs can generate high<span id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>quality synthetic data that substantially improves the performance of smaller downstream models. Similarly, Chen et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>)</cite> leveraged synthetic data generated by LLMs to compensate for real and rare samples. In our work, we completely utilized the data prepared by using large language models, specifically GPT-4o-mini. When the information of the chessboard and the position of each move are provided, large language models can accordingly give a rating for that move. However, a significant challenge arises from their failure to represent a multi-digit floating-point number when rating. Thus, SGGA is put forward to decompose these scores into the probability distribution of the graph. Our next move and placement selections are based on these models, our hybrid model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the section of Experiment and Analysis <a href="#S4" title="4 Experiment and Analysis ‣ Resource-constrained Amazons chess decision framework integrating large language models and graph attention This work was supported by the National Key Research and Development Project of China under Grant (No. 2020YFA0714300), National Natural Science Foundation of China under Grant (No. 61833005 and 12061088), the Open Project of Key Laboratory of Transport Industry of Comprehensive Transportation Theory (Nanjing Modern Multimodal Transportation Laboratory) under Grant MTF2023004, the China Postdoctoral Science Foundation under Grant 2024T170129 and Grant GZC20240261." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we provide results comparison, indicating that without finetuning LLMs and simply leveraging the graph structure derived from MCTS, LLMs can still generate data suitable for training deep learning models. Significantly, our SGGA plays a key role.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Application Stage</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our model is tested in the Game of Amazons. The two-step rule makes it different from other board games, resulting in a greatly increased search space. Under these circumstances, the cost of searching deeper nodes will be exponential. By applying this model to Game of the Amazons, the goal is to test the effectiveness of our model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For application stage, UCT-AE model is still used to strike the balance between exploitation and exploration. What makes it different from training stage is that SGGA is integrated with GAT-AE to comprehensively make the optimal decision.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Decision Strategy</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The decision strategy between two decisions needs thorough future research. In practical usage, the responsibility lies with players, while the final decision lies in their overall objective value randomly in this paper.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiment and Analysis</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this part, our experimental results and analysis are presented in detail, divided into three parts. First, the loss Analysis is presented, particularly focusing on the comparison between MCELoss for movement and placement. Second, the main experiment against GPT-4o-mini is conducted to justify the effectiveness of our hybrid model. Finally, ablation studies against various baseline models (UCTS-AE, SGGA, GAT-AE) were conducted to comprehensively evaluate the competitive strength and the necessity of each component, as summarized in Fig.<a href="#S4.F15" title="Fig. 15 ‣ 4.4 Overall Results ‣ 4 Experiment and Analysis ‣ Resource-constrained Amazons chess decision framework integrating large language models and graph attention This work was supported by the National Key Research and Development Project of China under Grant (No. 2020YFA0714300), National Natural Science Foundation of China under Grant (No. 61833005 and 12061088), the Open Project of Key Laboratory of Transport Industry of Comprehensive Transportation Theory (Nanjing Modern Multimodal Transportation Laboratory) under Grant MTF2023004, the China Postdoctoral Science Foundation under Grant 2024T170129 and Grant GZC20240261." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To validate our model, we constructed the Game of Amazons system. The experiments were conducted on the equipment of AMD Radeon(TM) 780M and NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4060 Laptop GPU. Compared to other models, our hardware setup is relatively modest, which highlights our algorithm’s ability to deliver excellent performance on lower<span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>end hardware, substantially lowering the barrier to entry, enabling a wider range of users to deploy and run the system without high<span id="S4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>end devices, and fostering broader adoption and usage.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Loss Analysis</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Figs.<a href="#S4.F7" title="Fig. 7 ‣ 4.1 Loss Analysis ‣ 4 Experiment and Analysis ‣ Resource-constrained Amazons chess decision framework integrating large language models and graph attention This work was supported by the National Key Research and Development Project of China under Grant (No. 2020YFA0714300), National Natural Science Foundation of China under Grant (No. 61833005 and 12061088), the Open Project of Key Laboratory of Transport Industry of Comprehensive Transportation Theory (Nanjing Modern Multimodal Transportation Laboratory) under Grant MTF2023004, the China Postdoctoral Science Foundation under Grant 2024T170129 and Grant GZC20240261." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>-<a href="#S4.F8" title="Fig. 8 ‣ 4.1 Loss Analysis ‣ 4 Experiment and Analysis ‣ Resource-constrained Amazons chess decision framework integrating large language models and graph attention This work was supported by the National Key Research and Development Project of China under Grant (No. 2020YFA0714300), National Natural Science Foundation of China under Grant (No. 61833005 and 12061088), the Open Project of Key Laboratory of Transport Industry of Comprehensive Transportation Theory (Nanjing Modern Multimodal Transportation Laboratory) under Grant MTF2023004, the China Postdoctoral Science Foundation under Grant 2024T170129 and Grant GZC20240261." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, the training loss curves for the movement and placement tasks are plotted, respectively. The blue curves show the raw MSELoss values, while the red curves are smoothed with a moving average using a window size of 50. For the movement task, the loss declines rapidly from approximately 0.04 to 0.01 within the first 500 iterations before leveling off. In contrast, the placement task’s loss drops from about 0.03 to 0.01 over the first 500 iterations but then plateaus, showing little further improvement.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In detail, while the initial loss in our work appears lower than that in other works, it should be noted that one limitation of our loss curve is that it does not include the very early stages of training. However, this omission does not affect the overall analysis, as the convergence behavior of our model remains stable and consistent with a great difference in these two figures. For example, during the plateau phase, MSE loss of the placement task shows pronounced fluctuations, while that of the movement task oscillates much less, indicating a more stable convergence behavior and lower variance than that of the placement task loss. To illustrate it numerically, the variance of two curves is computed. After epoch 50, we sample the loss values for the movement and placement actions. The variance of the loss of movement action reaches <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8.0\times 10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>8.0</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8.0\times 10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math> while that of the loss of placement action reaches <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.1\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.1\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>. An F<span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>test yielded F(<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>) = <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{value}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{value}</annotation></semantics></math>, p = 0.035, indicating a statistically significant difference in variances at the <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.05</annotation></semantics></math> level.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.10512v1/loss1.png" id="S4.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:286/173;" width="286" height="173" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 7: </span>Training loss of movement action</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.10512v1/loss2.png" id="S4.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:286/173;" width="286" height="173" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 8: </span>Training loss of movement action of placement action</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Also, this contrast suggests that the model is capable of learning meaningful representations for movement, whereas the learned features for placement lack generalizability under the same model architecture. This discrepancy can be partly attributed to the different strategies employed in selecting the next movement and placement. Specifically, SGGA was adopted for movement selection, while a simple weighted random selection was used for placement. This difference in strategy introduces a bias in data selection. The results suggest that our use of SGGA effectively reduces the variance in node selection. One of the explanations suggests that SGGA may enhance the overall quality of the training data since the policy was the only variable modified.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Main Experiment: Competitions Against Teacher Model GPT-4o-mini</h3>

<figure id="S4.F9.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:169.1pt;"><img src="2603.10512v1/AI.png" id="S4.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:210/129;" width="210" height="129" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 9: </span>Comparison between Hybrid Model and GPT-4o-mini under N=30 and N=50 search constraints</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fig.<a href="#S4.F9.fig1" title="Fig. 9 ‣ 4.2 Main Experiment: Competitions Against Teacher Model GPT-4o-mini ‣ 4 Experiment and Analysis ‣ Resource-constrained Amazons chess decision framework integrating large language models and graph attention This work was supported by the National Key Research and Development Project of China under Grant (No. 2020YFA0714300), National Natural Science Foundation of China under Grant (No. 61833005 and 12061088), the Open Project of Key Laboratory of Transport Industry of Comprehensive Transportation Theory (Nanjing Modern Multimodal Transportation Laboratory) under Grant MTF2023004, the China Postdoctoral Science Foundation under Grant 2024T170129 and Grant GZC20240261." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> illustrates the performance of our hybrid model against GPT-4o-mini. In alignment with our resource-constrained objective, the primary goal of this experiment was not merely to achieve victory, but rather to evaluate the minimum search scale required by our framework to surpass the generative decision-making of a high-performance LLM. As shown, with a highly restricted budget of only N=30 nodes, our model already reaches a competitive win rate of 45.0%. When the search limit is slightly extended to N=50, the win rate significantly increases to 66.5%, effectively crossing the threshold of victory.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This result highlights the efficiency of our hybrid framework: it requires an extremely small computational footprint to outperform its "teacher" model. Notably, the differences between this experiment and those preceding it lie in the specific search iterations chosen to probe the efficiency boundary against the powerful capabilities of GPT. Even at N=50, the computational scale of our hybrid model remains orders of magnitude smaller than that of GPT. Furthermore, it was observed that GPT-4o-mini often struggled to accurately track the coordinates of the four ’Amazons’, necessitating explicit location prompts and strict rule-based constraints to prevent illegal moves. In summary, our hybrid model successfully distills evaluation knowledge from GPT but ultimately outperforms it by leveraging the structural reasoning of GAT and the stochastic optimization of SGGA, demonstrating a clear "weak-to-strong" generalization in low-resource settings.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Ablation Studies: Competitions Against Different Baselines</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, the results of our hybrid model against various models were shown and analyzed. Under extensive node-searching conditions, we repeated the above experiments to ensure extensive node-searching would not disrupt the trend. Typically, the hybrid model may not necessarily enhance performance and could even worsen the situation due to inconsistencies in decision-making if their representation spaces are either identical or incomplete. To testify the effectiveness, the ideal way is to exhibit the complementarity of the hybrid model. Therefore, we conducted competitions between our hybrid model and other models, including UCTS-AE, GAT-AE, and SGGA, to justify the complementarity. To illustrate that this result still holds with different numbers of nodes searched, we changed the termination condition from 20 to 30 nodes and repeated the experiment. For UCTS-AE model, we use the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.01, and the mean squared error (MSE) loss function as the optimization criterion; For GAT-AE model, we use the RMSprop optimizer with a learning rate of 0.0001, and the Smooth L1 loss as the optimization criterion. The settings of these two models in our hybrid model are identical to those of UCTS-AE and GAT-AE models.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F11" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F11.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:169.1pt;"><img src="2603.10512v1/N20.png" id="S4.F11.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:210/129;" width="210" height="129" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 10: </span>Overall Performance of Hybrid Model under 20 numbers of node searched</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F11.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:169.1pt;"><img src="2603.10512v1/N30.png" id="S4.F11.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:210/129;" width="210" height="129" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 11: </span>Overall Performance of Hybrid Model under 30 numbers of node searched</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To comprehensively illustrate the overall trend, Fig.<a href="#S4.F11" title="Fig. 11 ‣ 4.3 Ablation Studies: Competitions Against Different Baselines ‣ 4 Experiment and Analysis ‣ Resource-constrained Amazons chess decision framework integrating large language models and graph attention This work was supported by the National Key Research and Development Project of China under Grant (No. 2020YFA0714300), National Natural Science Foundation of China under Grant (No. 61833005 and 12061088), the Open Project of Key Laboratory of Transport Industry of Comprehensive Transportation Theory (Nanjing Modern Multimodal Transportation Laboratory) under Grant MTF2023004, the China Postdoctoral Science Foundation under Grant 2024T170129 and Grant GZC20240261." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> and Fig.<a href="#S4.F11" title="Fig. 11 ‣ 4.3 Ablation Studies: Competitions Against Different Baselines ‣ 4 Experiment and Analysis ‣ Resource-constrained Amazons chess decision framework integrating large language models and graph attention This work was supported by the National Key Research and Development Project of China under Grant (No. 2020YFA0714300), National Natural Science Foundation of China under Grant (No. 61833005 and 12061088), the Open Project of Key Laboratory of Transport Industry of Comprehensive Transportation Theory (Nanjing Modern Multimodal Transportation Laboratory) under Grant MTF2023004, the China Postdoctoral Science Foundation under Grant 2024T170129 and Grant GZC20240261." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> display the combined win-rate curves of our hybrid model against all three baselines under 20 and 30 nodes searched, respectively. In the following, we analyze the specific comparison for each baseline.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F12" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.10512v1/HvU.png" id="S4.F12.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:286/175;" width="286" height="175" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 12: </span>Comparison between Hybrid Model and UCTS-AE</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hybrid Model vs UCTS-AE:</span> UCTS-AE serves as a powerful nonlinear transformation module within our system. As illustrated in Fig.<a href="#S4.F12" title="Fig. 12 ‣ 4.3 Ablation Studies: Competitions Against Different Baselines ‣ 4 Experiment and Analysis ‣ Resource-constrained Amazons chess decision framework integrating large language models and graph attention This work was supported by the National Key Research and Development Project of China under Grant (No. 2020YFA0714300), National Natural Science Foundation of China under Grant (No. 61833005 and 12061088), the Open Project of Key Laboratory of Transport Industry of Comprehensive Transportation Theory (Nanjing Modern Multimodal Transportation Laboratory) under Grant MTF2023004, the China Postdoctoral Science Foundation under Grant 2024T170129 and Grant GZC20240261." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>, our model achieves a 79.5% win rate (159/200) against the UCTS<span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>AE baseline when limited to 20 nodes. Moreover, the win-rate curve remains remarkably stable across games, demonstrating that using the same evaluation function, our architectural modifications lead to more consistent and effective play strategy. The remaining 20.5% of losses can largely be attributed to occasional failures in identifying decisive moves during critical game states, suggesting a promising avenue for further refinement. Under 30 numbers of nodes searched, our hybrid model achieves a 73.5% win rate (147/200) against the UCTS<span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text">-</span>AE baseline, which suggests that the model with more nodes searched still has its advantage. However, whether it’s effective with a large number of nodes searched is still unknown.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F13" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.10512v1/HvS.png" id="S4.F13.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:286/175;" width="286" height="175" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 13: </span>Comparison between Hybrid Model and SGGA</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hybrid Model vs SGGA:</span> SGGA model serves as a model used for capturing the relationship of neighboring nodes in the Monte Carlo Tree. As illustrated in Fig.<a href="#S4.F13" title="Fig. 13 ‣ 4.3 Ablation Studies: Competitions Against Different Baselines ‣ 4 Experiment and Analysis ‣ Resource-constrained Amazons chess decision framework integrating large language models and graph attention This work was supported by the National Key Research and Development Project of China under Grant (No. 2020YFA0714300), National Natural Science Foundation of China under Grant (No. 61833005 and 12061088), the Open Project of Key Laboratory of Transport Industry of Comprehensive Transportation Theory (Nanjing Modern Multimodal Transportation Laboratory) under Grant MTF2023004, the China Postdoctoral Science Foundation under Grant 2024T170129 and Grant GZC20240261." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>, the win rate of our hybrid model against the SGGA model reaches 58.5% (117/200) under 20 nodes searched. In SGGA, while the selection of the tree nodes relies on the objective value, the distribution of the tree focuses on the neighboring nodes of one target node. Simply put, when a node has fewer neighbor nodes, it will be more likely to be the next generation. SGGA is a purely stochastic algorithm and the result shows that the model relying simply on probability itself fails to perform as well as that with a structural algorithm, underlying the effectiveness of our proposed model. Besides, our hybrid model achieves a 59.0% win rate (118/200) against the SGGA baseline under 30 numbers of nodes searched, suggesting that more nodes searched will not damage the effectiveness of our models. But similarly, whether it’s effective with a large number of nodes searched is still unknown.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F14" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.10512v1/HvG.png" id="S4.F14.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:286/175;" width="286" height="175" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 14: </span>Comparison between Hybrid Model and GAT-AE</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hybrid Model vs GAT-AE:</span> The GAT-AE model serves as a model used for capturing the structure of the Monte Carlo Tree, which compensates for the drawback of the SGGA model. As is shown in Fig.<a href="#S4.F14" title="Fig. 14 ‣ 4.3 Ablation Studies: Competitions Against Different Baselines ‣ 4 Experiment and Analysis ‣ Resource-constrained Amazons chess decision framework integrating large language models and graph attention This work was supported by the National Key Research and Development Project of China under Grant (No. 2020YFA0714300), National Natural Science Foundation of China under Grant (No. 61833005 and 12061088), the Open Project of Key Laboratory of Transport Industry of Comprehensive Transportation Theory (Nanjing Modern Multimodal Transportation Laboratory) under Grant MTF2023004, the China Postdoctoral Science Foundation under Grant 2024T170129 and Grant GZC20240261." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>, the win rate of our hybrid model against the GAT-AE model reaches around 62.0% (124/200) under 20 numbers of node searched. While training such a complicated model usually requires expensive datasets, our algorithm utilizes inexpensive data generated by LLMs and shows its feasibility. Under 30 numbers of node searched, our hybrid model achieves a 57.5% win rate (115/200) against the GAT-AE baseline. The little decline in win rate can be partially attributed to the incompleteness of training if the GAT-AE model. However, the result still shows its effectiveness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In conclusion, our hybrid model is effective under resource-limited settings with both 20 node searched and 30 node searched.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Overall Results</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the overall strength of our proposed framework, we present Fig.<a href="#S4.F15" title="Fig. 15 ‣ 4.4 Overall Results ‣ 4 Experiment and Analysis ‣ Resource-constrained Amazons chess decision framework integrating large language models and graph attention This work was supported by the National Key Research and Development Project of China under Grant (No. 2020YFA0714300), National Natural Science Foundation of China under Grant (No. 61833005 and 12061088), the Open Project of Key Laboratory of Transport Industry of Comprehensive Transportation Theory (Nanjing Modern Multimodal Transportation Laboratory) under Grant MTF2023004, the China Postdoctoral Science Foundation under Grant 2024T170129 and Grant GZC20240261." class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> to better illustrate the comparison between our hybrid model against the teacher model (GPT-4o-mini) and various ablation baselines (UCTS-AE, SGGA, GAT-AE). We also compute standard error confidence intervals based on the empirical binomial proportion of the final win rates.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F15" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.10512v1/win_rate.png" id="S4.F15.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/229;" width="381" height="229" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 15: </span>Win Rate Comparison Experiments</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The models were each robustly evaluated over 200 games. In the figure, the win rates of our hybrid model against various opponents are displayed across different node search limits (N=20, N=30, and N=50). Against the ablation baselines (UCTS-AE, SGGA, GAT-AE), our model achieves win rates ranging from 57.5% to 79.5% under N=20 and N=30 search conditions. Notably, in the main experiment against the teacher model (GPT-4o-mini), our hybrid framework achieves a 45.0% win rate at N=30, which significantly improves to 66.5% when the search limit is increased to N=50. The results demonstrate that our algorithm can effectively enhance the system’s strength under rather small search iterations and resource-limited settings, thereby validating its improvements over traditional models in both decision-making and evaluation functions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Discussion</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Weak Supervision and Beyond</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is worth noting that while established handcrafted engines (e.g., Invader) represent the current ceiling of performance in Amazons, they rely on decades of domain-specific heuristics that are hard to transfer to other problems. In contrast, our work focuses on the <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">generalizability of learning from weak supervision</span>. We acknowledge that in terms of absolute ELO rating, our model currently trails behind these specialized engines. However, our framework demonstrates <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">autonomous improvement</span> purely from natural language prompts, validating a path for domains where expert heuristics are unavailable.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our results align with the findings of Burns et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Burns et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, demonstrating that a strong student model (our Hybrid Framework) can be elicited from a weak supervisor (GPT-4o-mini). The observed performance gap (Student <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math> Teacher) confirms that our architectural components—specifically the GAT-AE and SGGA—act as effective <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">information bottlenecks</span>. By constraining the learning process to topological features, the model filters out the supervisor’s inconsistencies (hallucinations) while retaining generalizable strategic logic. This suggests that our framework is not merely a game solver, but a viable prototype for deploying evolved AI agents in domains where only imperfect or noisy supervision is available.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Robustness against Hallucinations</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A key concern in using LLMs for data augmentation is the presence of noise, such as illegal or suboptimal moves. Our ablation studies reveal that the Graph Attention mechanism is critical for mitigating this issue. Since the GAT is constrained to learn graph-topological features of the board, it cannot memorize the random noise inherent in the LLM’s raw output. Instead, it captures consistent, underlying strategic patterns (e.g., territory connectivity). This allows the model to “denoise” the training signal, effectively correcting the teacher’s errors during the distillation process.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>CONCLUSION AND FUTURE WORK</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, a resource-limited hybrid model was proposed for an intelligent system by integrating SGGA with GAT-AE, which fully captures the value and structure of Monte Carlo Tree. To the best of our knowledge, we are the first to do a resource-limited task on the Game of the Amazons. A deep learning model was trained using inexpensive data generated by LLMs, and its effectiveness was validated by testing three models against our hybrid model. Our algorithm has observed a radical success in combating classic models and GPT-4o-mini with rather small search iterations. Looking ahead, there are two areas for future research. First, we need to clarify how to determine if the model has been fully trained. It is challenging to assess this solely through the loss value, as the values are relatively close to each other. Second, developing a decision strategy for final choice is vital. While a choice was made based on randomness in this paper, such research will further enhance the intelligent system and help explore the upper limits of our algorithm.</p>
</div>
</section>
